<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<task id="140" project="unified-access-control-r2">
  <metadata>
    <title>NEW FEATURE — contact-side Grant Access: a Collaborate/Full Access contact grants colleagues of their own organization, capped at their own level, from the external SPA</title>
    <phase>Register-driven (authored 2026-09-30, session 27; grant-model group; C4 owner decision)</phase>
    <gate>after-135-136-138-139-141-013</gate>
    <status>in-progress</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>New access-minting write path reachable by CIAM tokens; new routes, new endpoint filter, new Dataverse column, external SPA wiring; tags bff-api/auth/security.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>opus</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>First route that lets a NON-Dataverse principal mint access; the authorization must be derived entirely from the BFF's own principal model with no Dataverse backstop, so every rule (org scope, cap, Secure/Limited/Restricted, deny list, no-downgrade) is security-critical.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>high</effort>
    <parallel-group>grant-model</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>false</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>Extends GrantExternalAccessEndpoint's shared grant core (modified by 138 and 139; also 137 and 142) and ExternalAccessEndpoints.cs route mapping; consumes the CallerPrincipal shape that tasks 135/136 change; edits ExternalParticipationService.cs (131, 132, 137), tests/Spaarke.ArchTests/RouteAuthorizationGuardTests.cs (130 and most register-driven auth tasks) and AccessGrantModal.tsx (138, 139, 066/067). Merge serially and run /conflict-check first.</parallel-reason>
    <deps>135, 136, 138, 139, 141, 013 (131 transitively via 135)</deps>
    <tags>bff-api, auth, security, dataverse, frontend, testing</tags>
    <estimated-effort>2-3 days</estimated-effort>
    <origin>Owner decision session 27 round 1 (C4: "a contact user with collaborate or full access ... should not be prevented from using the 'grant access' feature") and round 2 Q2 ("a contact grants only to contacts of their own organization, at or below their own level, never organization-wide").</origin>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>Build contact-side Grant Access. A contact holding Collaborate or Full Access on a project, matter or work assignment may grant access to other contacts of their own organization, at or below their own level, never organization-wide, never on Restricted records, and on Secure/Limited records only by direct grant. Today contacts cannot grant at all: the management routes are workforce-only and the external SPA's "Invite User" button is dead.</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE platform developer — BFF external collaboration surface (/api/v1/external, CallerPrincipal) and the external SPA. Follow ADRs strictly.</role>

  <goal>
    (1) CIAM-reachable routes on the /api/v1/external group let a contact principal grant, list the grants they issued, and revoke them, each gated by a route-level endpoint filter that enforces the owner's contact-grantor rules.
    (2) Grants are written through the SAME shared grant core as /grant, with expiry defaulting to today + 90 days and a contact-typed audit of who granted. Task 138's policy check (inside the core) applies unchanged, and task 139's ceiling method (applied in handlers) is called by this route's handler.
    (3) The external SPA's Contacts tab "Invite User" works for Collaborate and Full Access contacts, offers only levels at or below theirs, and shows the grants they issued with a working Revoke.
    (4) A manual live gate on spaarkedev1 proves the feature end to end with a CIAM contact.
  </goal>

  <context>
    <background>
OWNER RULES (BINDING — session 27):
- A contact with **Collaborate or Full Access** on a record may grant; a **View Only** contact may not.
- Grantees: **other contacts of the grantor's OWN organization only** (Q2). Never organization-wide.
- Level: **at or below the grantor's own level** (Q1 cap).
- **Restricted** records: no contact access at all → a contact can never grant there.
- **Secure** records (and Limited): only named, direct grants count for contacts → the grant written is direct by construction, and the grantor's own level on such a record comes from their DIRECT grant only.
- Expiry: a grant with no expiry gets today + 90 days (binding directive). A failed revoke returns a message, not a bare 500 (binding directive).

WHY CONTACTS CANNOT GRANT TODAY (verified 2026-09-30):
- The management group `/api/v1/external-access` (`Api/ExternalAccess/ExternalAccessEndpoints.cs:123-126`) uses the workforce default scheme + `DelegationRuleFilter`, whose check is an OBO `RetrievePrincipalAccess` (`Api/ExternalAccess/DelegationRuleFilter.cs:155-179`). A contact is not a Dataverse principal and a CIAM token cannot be exchanged OBO, so neither the scheme nor the check can ever admit a contact.
- The collaboration group `/api/v1/external` (`ExternalAccessEndpoints.cs:54-57`, policy `AuthPolicies.ExternalCollaboration` registered at `Infrastructure/DI/AuthorizationModule.cs:351`) accepts CIAM and workforce tokens and resolves a plane-agnostic `CallerPrincipal` (`Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/CallerPrincipalResolver.cs:93-157`: `Plane`, `ContactId`, `SystemUserId`, per-root rights maps) — but has no grant route.
- The external SPA's Contacts tab (`src/client/external-spa/src/pages/ProjectPage.tsx:279-310`) shows "Invite User" for Full Access only (`canInvite`, `src/client/external-spa/src/hooks/useAccessLevel.ts:75-78`; dialog guard `src/client/external-spa/src/components/InviteUserDialog.tsx:164-168`). It posts to `POST /api/v1/external-access/invite` (`src/client/external-spa/src/auth/bff-client.ts:310-322`), which a CIAM token cannot authenticate to, and `/invite` writes no grant anyway (`Api/ExternalAccess/InviteExternalUserEndpoint.cs:9-29`). `bff-client.ts:274-298` also carries `grantAccess`/`revokeAccess` calls to the same unreachable group. Note: `ProjectPage.tsx:282` computes its OWN `canUserInvite = accessLevel === AccessLevel.FullAccess` inline and does not call `canInvite`, so fixing `useAccessLevel.ts` alone changes nothing on screen. The mock service matches every POST under `/api/v1/external-access/` (`mocks/mock-service.ts:133-134`).
- Only ProjectPage has a Contacts tab. The external SPA has no matter or work-assignment detail page (`src/client/external-spa/src/pages/`; WorkAssignmentsWidget is a list widget). The routes serve all three root types; the SPA wiring covers the one surface that exists. If a matter or work-assignment detail page with a Contacts tab exists at execution time, wire it too (no scope cut).
- The grant table (`sprk_externalrecordaccess`, live metadata 2026-09-30) has `sprk_grantedby` as a SYSTEMUSER lookup only — there is no column that can record a contact grantor.

REUSE MAP (CLAUDE.md §11):
- Write core: `GrantExternalAccessEndpoint.CreateGrantAsync` (`Api/ExternalAccess/GrantExternalAccessEndpoint.cs:186-383`) — upsert, duplicate collapse, +90 default, expired-row 409, cache invalidation. After 138 it also carries the Restricted/Limited/Secure write-time policy. Task 139 puts the ceiling, never-lower and deny checks in the /grant and /invite-and-grant HANDLERS (the Q5 auto-grant of task 142 has no human grantor), so this route's handler calls the same methods.
- Grantor level: the authorization-grade function task 139 adds (Read+Write+Delete → Full; Read+Write → Collaborate; Read → View Only). A contact's rights map is always one of R / R|C|W / R|C|W|D (`ExternalAccessLevels.ToAccessRights`, `Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/ExternalCallerContext.cs:207-213`), so the same function applies exactly.
- The caller's rights on a root: `RightsForRoot` pattern (`Api/ExternalAccess/ExternalProjectDataEndpoints.cs:467-474`) over `CallerPrincipal` — which, after tasks 135/136 (per the orchestrator's dependency statement; re-derive their exact scope from their own POMLs), carries post-veto, rights-based answers on BOTH planes (Secure/Limited → direct grant only, Restricted → none).
- Org membership: the ONE junction reader is `ExternalParticipationService.QueryActiveOrgIdsAsync` (public virtual, `Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/ExternalParticipationService.cs:1081`; query at `:1096-1124`). `AccessibleRecordSetService.ReadActiveOrgMembershipsAsync` (`AccessibleRecordSetService.cs:486`) only wraps it. Two properties make it unfit AS IS for a grant-eligibility decision, so extend it (a parameter or overload on the same method, not a copy):
  1. It bounds on `statecode eq 0` only and deliberately NOT on `sprk_enddate`. notes/task-043-org-expansion-term.md §4.1 records why: the reader is shared by an additive term and a veto, whose fail directions are inverted. Eligibility to be GRANTED is additive, so it must be tightened: an ended membership does not qualify. Verify live whether the junction also carries a start-date column; if it does, bound on it too.
  2. It swallows every fault into an EMPTY list (`:1112-1117`, `:1127-1131`), which here would read as "no organization". The contact route must distinguish a fault: 503 with a message, never 403 no_organization.
- Deny list (No Access List): task 139 adds the narrow single-subject, single-record write-time entry point over `AccessibleRecordSetService.ResolveDenyVetoAsync` (`AccessibleRecordSetService.cs:596`; task 039 / FR-23) and the reasonCode `sdap.access.grant.grantee_denied`. Reuse both; do not add a second deny check.
- Never-lower: task 139's refusal `sdap.access.grant.would_lower_existing` (409), reused verbatim.
- Revoke: `ExternalGrantLifecycle` (`RetrieveRowAsync`, `DeactivateAsync`) as used by `RevokeExternalAccessEndpoint`.
- CIAM onboarding: `InviteExternalUserEndpoint.ProvisionAsync` (cert-credential provisioner — ADR-028 A4 compliant) as composed by `InviteAndGrantExternalUserEndpoint.cs:97-98`.
    </background>
