  <constraints>
    <constraint source="owner 2026-09-30 C4 + Q2">Scope: every new route. The caller MUST be a contact principal (CIAM contact, or workforce contact-only). A workforce SYSTEMUSER principal is refused 403 `sdap.access.contact_grant.use_manage_access` with a message pointing to Manage Access (systemusers grant through the Write-gated management group).</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner 2026-09-30 C4">Scope: grant + revoke. The grantor's level on the target root (from the CallerPrincipal rights map, via task 139's grantor-level function) must be Collaborate or Full Access; View Only or none → 403 `sdap.access.contact_grant.level_insufficient`. Out-of-scope root and insufficient level are indistinguishable to the caller (same status, same code family), mirroring RightsForRoot's "caller cannot tell which happened".</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner Q1">Scope: grant. The written level is capped at the grantor's level by task 139's ceiling method, called in this handler over the grantor's CallerPrincipal rights for the root (narrow, never refuse, per 139's stated decision). The response reports `grantedLevel` and `narrowed`.</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner Q2">Scope: grant. The grantee is ONE contact; the request DTO has NO organization-grantee field (an org-wide grant is structurally impossible on this route). The grantee must be an ACTIVE contact holding an ACTIVE membership (junction statecode 0, `sprk_enddate` absent or not passed, and a start date reached if the junction carries one) in at least one organization in which the GRANTOR also holds an active membership under the same test.
- A grantee who fails this is refused 422 `sdap.access.contact_grant.grantee_not_in_organization`.
- A grantor with no active organization is refused 403 `sdap.access.contact_grant.no_organization`.
- A membership read FAULT is 503 `sdap.access.contact_grant.membership_unreadable` with a message. It is never folded into no_organization or grantee_not_in_organization.
- Self-grant is refused 400.
- The contact route NEVER creates, alters, activates or ends a `sprk_contactorganization` row (see escalation trigger 6).</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner round 2 item 3 / task 138">Scope: grant. Restricted → refused. After task 135 a contact's post-veto level on a Restricted root is None, so ContactGrantorFilter refuses first (403 level_insufficient). The core's 422 `sdap.access.grant.record_restricted` (task 138) is the second, independent refusal, and both are tested. Secure/Limited → allowed only because the write is a direct grant, and only when the grantor's own level there is from a direct grant (guaranteed by 135's parity; add a test that pins it).</constraint>
    <constraint source="FR-23 (No Access List)">Scope: grant. A grantee who is on the record's No Access List (as a contact or through an organization) is refused 422 `sdap.access.grant.grantee_denied` at write time, through task 139's write-time deny entry point. It is never written and left for the read-time veto.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project (no proxy revocation or proxy extension)">Scope: grant. A contact grantor never changes access someone else issued.
- If the grantee has NO active row on (root × grantee), the route creates one (capped level, +90 default expiry, contact grantor stamped).
- If the grantee has an active row that THIS caller issued (contact-grantor column = caller), the route may raise its level up to the cap and lengthen its expiry. It never lowers the level; a lower request is refused 409 `sdap.access.grant.would_lower_existing` (task 139's code). It never shortens the expiry.
- If the grantee has an active row issued by ANYONE ELSE (a systemuser, or another contact), the route leaves it untouched: no level change, no expiry change, no re-stamp. It answers 409 `sdap.access.contact_grant.managed_elsewhere` with a message ("{name} already has access to this record that was granted by someone else; ask them or the record's team to change it").
- Why: re-stamping a row someone else issued would let the contact then REVOKE it (revoke is issuer-scoped), which is proxy revocation. Extending its expiry would override an operator's deliberate time bound. See escalation trigger 3 for the alternative.</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner binding directive (+90)">Scope: grant. An absent expiry defaults to today + 90 days via the shared core; a past expiry is refused 400 `sdap.access.grant.expiry_in_past` (existing ValidateRequestedExpiry).</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner binding directive (message, not 500)">Scope: revoke. A contact may revoke only rows whose contact-grantor column is the caller, on a root where the caller still holds Collaborate or Full Access. Any failure (row not found, not theirs, Dataverse fault) returns ProblemDetails with a message and reason code — never a bare 500. Not-found and not-theirs are indistinguishable (same 404 + code) so the route is not an access-record oracle.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-008">Scope: authorization. The contact-grantor rules are enforced in a route-level endpoint filter (`ContactGrantorFilter`) attached to every new route, deny-by-default for any unmapped request type (mirror DelegationRuleFilter's default-deny switch); the handler re-validates the root it writes (a handler must not trust a pipeline it cannot see).</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-028 A1 / A4 + C7">Scope: onboarding. CIAM onboarding (ProvisionAsync) runs ONLY when the grantor is on the CIAM plane; a workforce contact-only grantor may grant only to EXISTING contacts (their colleagues sign in with company SSO — identity binding is the C7 task's scope). No new `.WithClientSecret(...)` anywhere.</constraint>
    <constraint source="DATAVERSE-WRITE-PATH-ARCHITECTURE WP-1/WP-3">Scope: the write. All grant rows go through the shared BFF core; the contact grantor is recorded in a new contact-typed lookup on sprk_externalrecordaccess (e.g. `sprk_grantedbycontact`), and `sprk_grantedby` (systemuser) stays empty for contact-issued grants. Every write logs `[EXT-CONTACT-GRANT]` with grantor contact id, grantee contact id, root type/id, requested level, granted level, narrowed, expiry.</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner binding directive (no Dataverse test in CI)">Scope: verification. Live proof is a MANUAL gate using existing test identities; no user relocation; no CI job calling Dataverse.</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner directive 2026-09-04 (FAILURE-MODES AP-13)">Scope: the new lookup column. On this project, schema work by the agent is CODE + DOCS only; live creation is an explicit OPERATOR step.
- The agent authors an idempotent, `-DryRun`-default script (e.g. scripts/Deploy-ExternalRecordAccessContactGrantor.ps1). It uses the raw Web API with the `MSCRM.SolutionUniqueName` header and the `sprk` publisher, in the shape of scripts/Deploy-AccessEventEntity.ps1, and authenticates with the operator's own az/pac token (no client secret).
- The agent never calls `mcp__dataverse__create_table`/`update_table` for it (AP-13: no publisher or solution parameter).
- After the operator runs the script, the agent reads the column back (`mcp__dataverse__describe` or a metadata GET) and asserts the `sprk_` prefix, the contact target, and membership in the SpaarkeCore solution.
- It updates src/solutions/SpaarkeCore/entities/sprk_externalrecordaccess/entity-schema.md.</constraint>
    <constraint source="CLAUDE.md §10">Scope: this task's PR. Placement Justification: in BFF (CallerPrincipal, grant core and CIAM onboarding all live there; bff-extensions.md criteria). Publish size measured per CLAUDE.md §10 (fresh master + fresh branch worktrees from SHORT paths, Compress-Archive both, equal file counts, both sizes + delta); ≤60 MB; no new HIGH CVE. Unconditional route mapping ⇒ unconditional registration of everything it injects.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-038">Scope: tests. No Mock&lt;HttpMessageHandler&gt;, no DI-registration or ctor null-check tests. Every negative has a positive twin differing in exactly one input (level, org membership, flag, principal kind).</constraint>
      <constraint source="main-session-review">Persist every contact-issued grant through 139's grant core, passing the contact grantor's effective level as the REQUIRED ceiling — never a direct write.</constraint>
</constraints>

